export const TERMS_BODY = "<p>These Terms of Service are a binding agreement between you and Westbridge Inc., a company registered in Guyana (registration no. 17703) trading as Swift, of Lot 165 New Trafalgar Building, Waterloo Street, Georgetown, Demerara, Guyana (\"<b>Swift</b>\", \"we\", \"us\"), which operates the Swift app, websites and related services in Guyana (the \"<b>Platform</b>\"). By creating an account and ticking the acceptance box, you accept these Terms and confirm you have read our Privacy Policy. Your electronic acceptance forms a binding contract. If you do not agree, do not use the Platform.</p>\n<h2>1. Definitions</h2>\n<p>\"<b>Business</b>\" — an independent restaurant, supermarket, shop or service provider selling through the Platform. \"<b>Mover</b>\" — an independent delivery rider, courier or taxi driver offering transport through the Platform. \"<b>Partner</b>\" — a Business or a Mover. \"<b>Order</b>\" — a purchase, delivery, parcel, booking or ride you request through the Platform. \"<b>Handover</b>\" — the moment goods, a parcel or a completed service or ride is delivered to you.</p>\n<h2>2. What Swift is — and is not</h2>\n<p>Swift is a technology platform: it publishes Partners' listings, transmits Orders, matches deliveries and rides, and provides tracking, messaging and safety tools. Every Partner is an independent operator. Swift is <b>not</b> the seller of any goods, the provider of any ride, delivery or service, the employer, agent or joint venturer of any Partner, or a party to the contract formed between you and a Partner when an Order is accepted. Each Order is a direct contract between you and the relevant Business (for goods and services) and, for its carriage, the relevant Mover. Where these Terms limit Swift's responsibility, they never limit the responsibility a Partner owes you under the contract you form with them or under law.</p>\n<h2>3. Eligibility and your account</h2>\n<p>You must be at least 18 years old and capable of forming a binding contract. By creating an account you confirm that you are 18 or older; where the sign-up shows the \"I am 18 or older\" box, you confirm it by ticking that box. An account requires a phone number verified by SMS code. A customer needs no ID to place an Order or take a taxi ride; the app asks for a government-issued ID on file only after repeated failed deliveries or payments, or to use a high-value promotion. A customer never needs a photo. You agree to give accurate information, keep it current, use one account, and keep your sign-in ability to yourself. You are responsible for activity under your account until you tell us it has been compromised. One person may hold customer and Partner roles on the same account; the Partner roles carry additional obligations presented at Partner onboarding.</p>\n<h2>4. Payments are direct — Swift holds no money</h2>\n<p>Swift is software, not a payment service. You pay for an Order in cash at Handover or, where the Business offers it, directly into the Business's own MMG account under MMG's terms. Swift never holds, collects or transmits Order money, offers no wallet or stored value, and never asks for card numbers. Prices are the Business's own prices. Delivery fees and taxi fares are calculated by Swift and shown in the app before you confirm. Swift charges customers nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Taxi waiting charge.</b> When the app shows a waiting charge beside the taxi fare before you book, that charge applies to the ride: GY$300 for each full 10 minutes the car waits for you. Waiting is counted at the pickup, from when your driver arrives until your trip starts (the app checks the driver's location before the count starts, or you confirm the arrival yourself), and at any stop on the way, from when the car arrives until it leaves. Waiting at your final destination is never counted, and part of a 10-minute block is never charged. The Swift app shows the waiting time and the charge as they run, and the ride's receipt lists the waiting charge separately from the route fare. You pay it with your fare, directly to the driver. A ride that is cancelled, or that ends without the trip being completed, carries no waiting charge.</p>\n<h2>5. Placing an Order</h2>\n<p>Submitting an Order is an offer to the Business; the contract forms when the Business accepts it in the app. A Business can set a bookable service to confirm bookings automatically: if the app shows your booking as confirmed the moment you book it, that is the Business's acceptance, given in advance through that setting, and the contract forms at that moment. A Business may decline an Order and must tell you why through the app. If an item turns out to be unavailable, the app asks <b>you</b> to approve a substitution or that line is removed and, where you already paid by MMG, refunded by the Business — never swapped silently. Service bookings reserve the time slot shown; express delivery, where offered, buys priority handling.</p>\n<h2>6. Paying at Handover</h2>\n<p>Payment is due before or at Handover. Refusing to pay for what you ordered, or repeatedly failing to accept deliveries you requested, results in recorded strikes on your account and can restrict or end your access to cash-on-delivery or to the Platform. If a Business records in the app that you did not come to an appointment you booked, that record carries no fee and no strike; a person at Swift reviews repeated missed appointments and may contact you about them.</p>\n<h2>7. Cancellations and refunds</h2>\n<p>You can cancel an Order free while the Business has not yet accepted it. Once the Business accepts it, the Order can no longer be cancelled in the app: contact the Business to change or cancel it — the app shows the number the Business published. A ride, a parcel or a booking can be cancelled free at any time while nobody has accepted it, and for one minute after a driver, rider or provider accepts it; after that, contact your driver, rider or provider. An appointment is a time you book from a Business's listing. While Swift's instant appointment booking is switched on, an appointment the Business has confirmed (by accepting it, or automatically when you booked it) can be cancelled free in the app until 2 hours before it starts, and always for at least one minute after it was confirmed, instead of the one minute above; where the app offers it, you can also move it to another free time of the same service until 2 hours before it starts. After that, contact the Business to change or cancel it. The Business can also move your appointment, and you are told in the app when it does. A service job request can be cancelled free in the app until the job is booked; once it is booked, contact the provider to change or cancel it. You can still cancel free when no driver or rider was found for your Order, or when it is running very late, as the app shows. Once your Order is on its way, or you are in the taxi, it can no longer be cancelled. If you paid by MMG and the Business has already confirmed your payment, the app cannot cancel the Order at any stage, even before the Business accepts it: contact the Business to cancel it. <b>Swift never charges a cancellation fee.</b> If an Order you paid by MMG is cancelled, <b>the Business refunds you directly</b> on its own MMG — because Swift never holds the money, every refund of Order money is made by, and is the obligation of, the Partner who received it. If something goes wrong with a fulfilled Order, report it from the Order screen within 7 days. Swift reviews it with GPS and photo evidence and asks the Business to put it right; any refund of Order money is made by the Business that received it. Nothing in this section affects your rights under the Consumer Affairs Act 2011, including returning goods to, and getting refunds from, the Business.</p>\n<h2>8. Your conduct</h2>\n<p>You agree not to: (a) break the law through the Platform or order anything unlawful; (b) harass, threaten, discriminate against or endanger Partners, customers or staff; (c) place Orders you do not intend to honour, or manipulate ratings, promotions, referrals or the strike system; (d) trigger safety features falsely; (e) impersonate anyone or use another person's account or documents; (f) interfere with the Platform — no scraping, automated access, probing, reverse engineering, or circumventing technical or fee protections; (g) use Platform data for any purpose outside your own use of Swift; (h) order, sell, send or carry anything Swift prohibits: alcohol, tobacco and vapes, medicines and drugs, 18+ items, cooking gas and fuel, weapons and ammunition, fireworks and explosives, pesticides and controlled chemicals, protected wildlife, raw gold and precious minerals, lottery and gambling products, counterfeit goods, currency and payment cards, and letters or documents; or offer legal services. We may investigate violations and act under section 17.</p>\n<h2>9. Reviews, messages and other content</h2>\n<p>You may post ratings, reviews, reports and order-scoped chat messages. You keep ownership of what you write, and you grant Swift a non-exclusive, royalty-free, worldwide licence to host, display, moderate and share it as needed to operate the Platform (for example, showing your review on a storefront, or your first name and rating to a matched Partner). Content must be truthful, lawful and respectful; you are responsible for what you post, including under defamation law. We may remove content that breaks these rules and act on reports — every review and message carries a report action, and blocking a person stops their messages and stops Swift matching you with them. Our Child Safety Standards page states our zero-tolerance rules and reporting channel for child-safety concerns.</p>\n<h2>10. Safety features and their limits</h2>\n<p>Taxi rides include an in-app PIN handshake so you meet the right driver, live trip sharing, and an SOS button that alerts our operations team and your verified emergency contacts with your live location. These tools support your safety. Swift's safety team is not an emergency service and may not respond immediately; in an emergency call Police 911, Fire 912 or Ambulance 913 first. Misusing them — including false alarms — ends your access and may be reported to the authorities.</p>\n<h2>11. Partners</h2>\n<p>Partners subscribe to Swift for a flat weekly fee and keep 100% of what they earn — Swift charges Partners no commission on Orders. Partners are independent: they decide when to be open or online, are responsible for their goods, services, vehicles, licences, insurance and tax affairs, and are verified through document checks before operating. Partner accounts are subject to the additional terms presented at onboarding, to quality and safety standards, and to suspension for expired documents, non-payment of the subscription, or misconduct. Nothing in the Platform creates employment between Swift and any Partner.</p>\n<h2>12. Licence and intellectual property</h2>\n<p>We grant you a personal, revocable, non-transferable licence to use the Swift app and websites for their intended purpose. The Platform, its software, design, and the Swift name and marks belong to Swift or its licensors; Partner content belongs to Partners. No rights are granted except as stated. If you send us feedback or suggestions, we may use them without obligation.</p>\n<h2>13. Third-party services</h2>\n<p>Parts of the experience depend on third parties — for example MMG for direct payments between you and Partners, mapping providers, and SMS carriers. Their services are governed by their own terms, and Swift is not responsible for their acts, omissions or availability.</p>\n<h2>14. Availability and changes to the Platform</h2>\n<p>The Platform is provided on an \"as available\" basis: we work to keep it running, but we do not promise uninterrupted or error-free operation, that any Partner will be open or nearby, or that any feature will remain unchanged. We may modify, suspend or discontinue features with reasonable notice where the change is material. The app may update itself to fix problems or improve features.</p>\n<h2>15. Disclaimers</h2>\n<p>To the maximum extent permitted by law, Swift disclaims all warranties about the Platform not expressly stated in these Terms. The quality, safety, legality and fitness of goods, services, deliveries and rides are the responsibility of the Partner who provides them, and your statutory guarantees in respect of them — including under Guyana's consumer-protection law — operate against that Partner and are <b>not</b> affected by these Terms. Nothing in these Terms excludes or limits any right or remedy the law does not allow to be excluded or limited.</p>\n<h2>16. Limitation of liability</h2>\n<p>To the maximum extent permitted by law: (a) Swift is not liable for the acts or omissions of Partners or other users, or for loss arising from the transaction between you and a Partner; (b) Swift is not liable for indirect or consequential loss, loss of profit, business, or data; and (c) Swift's total liability to you for claims arising from the Platform's own role is limited to the greater of the amounts you paid Swift in the twelve months before the event and GYD 100,000. None of this excludes or limits liability for death or personal injury caused by Swift's own negligence, for fraud, or for anything else that cannot lawfully be excluded or limited.</p>\n<h2>17. Suspension and termination</h2>\n<p>You may stop using Swift at any time and may delete your account in the app (Profile → Personal data → Delete my account). We may suspend or terminate your access, with notice where practicable, for breach of these Terms, fraud, safety risk, legal requirement, or — for Partners — expired verification documents or subscription non-payment. Sections that by their nature should survive (including 9, 12, 15, 16, 18 and 19) survive termination, and records we are legally required to keep are retained as the Privacy Policy describes.</p>\n<h2>18. Indemnity</h2>\n<p>To the extent the law allows, you agree to compensate Swift for reasonable losses and costs caused by your fraud, your deliberate breach of these Terms, or unlawful content you post, except to the extent caused by Swift's own breach or negligence.</p>\n<h2>19. Governing law and disputes</h2>\n<p>These Terms are governed by the laws of the Co-operative Republic of Guyana, and the courts of Guyana have jurisdiction over disputes arising from them. Please contact us first — most issues are resolved through Help &amp; Support, which creates a tracked ticket a human answers. Nothing in these Terms limits your right to complain to or seek relief from any authority the law provides, including the Competition and Consumer Affairs Commission for consumer matters and, once the Data Protection Act is in force, the Data Protection Commissioner.</p>\n<h2>20. General</h2>\n<p>These Terms, the Privacy Policy, and the policies and Partner or advertiser terms referenced in them are the entire agreement between you and Swift about the Platform. If any provision is found invalid, the rest remain in force. A failure to enforce a provision is not a waiver of it. You may not assign your rights under these Terms; Swift may assign to an affiliate or a successor to its business, and material corporate changes affecting your data are handled as the Privacy Policy describes. Swift is not responsible for delay or failure caused by events beyond its reasonable control. These Terms are concluded in English.</p>\n<h2>21. Changes to these Terms</h2>\n<p>We announce material changes in the app at least 14 days before they take effect and ask you to accept them in the app. If you do not accept, you can stop using Swift and delete your account.</p>\n<h2>22. Contact</h2>\n<p>Westbridge Inc. (trading as Swift), registration no. 17703, Lot 165 New Trafalgar Building, Waterloo Street, Georgetown, Demerara, Guyana. Phone +592 716 3534. Email support@swiftgy.com; privacy matters privacy@swiftgy.com. The in-app <b>Help &amp; Support</b> section is the fastest channel.</p>";

export const PRIVACY_BODY = "<p>This policy explains what personal data Swift collects, why, on what legal basis, who it is shared with, how long it is kept, and the rights you have. Guyana's Data Protection Act 2023 has been passed but, as of 28 October 2026, has not been brought into force. Swift follows it now and will update this policy when it starts. We collect the minimum needed to run a delivery, ride and marketplace platform, and <b>we never sell your data</b>.</p>\n<h2>Who is responsible (data controller)</h2>\n<p>Westbridge Inc. (trading as Swift), registration no. 17703, Lot 165 New Trafalgar Building, Waterloo Street, Georgetown, Demerara, Guyana, is the data controller. Our data protection officer is the Data Protection Officer, Westbridge Inc., privacy@swiftgy.com. For anything about your privacy — access, correction, deletion or a complaint — email <b><a href=\"mailto:privacy@swiftgy.com\">privacy@swiftgy.com</a></b>. This inbox reaches us even if you can no longer sign in to the app, so you are never locked out of your rights. This policy covers customers, Partners (businesses and movers), advertiser contacts, and visitors to our websites.</p>\n<h2>1. What we collect</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Account:</b> your phone number (verified by SMS code), name, and optional email.</li>\n<li><b>A registration selfie (partners and advertisers):</b> riders, drivers, businesses, service providers and advertisers take one when they sign up, to deter account fraud; it becomes their in-app photo. A customer never needs a photo.</li>\n<li><b>Orders, rides and bookings:</b> what you ordered, addresses you save or enter, delivery instructions, and the trip/delivery history tied to your account.</li>\n<li><b>Location:</b> your device location while you use the app to set pickup/delivery points. For movers, live location while online — that is what powers dispatch and customer tracking. Background collection for movers happens only after a separate, explicit in-app consent, and stops when you go offline.</li>\n<li><b>Verification documents (partners and, where they add one, customers):</b> government ID and, for movers, licence, insurance and related documents. These are stored privately; in the app they are viewable only by our verification team through short-lived signed links, each access logged. Identity verification is done by people: our verification team checks each partner document and compares a partner's identity document with that partner's selfie. A customer's ID goes on file when it is uploaded, unless a simple automatic check holds it for a person (see AI processing in section 5); our team can look at it, and withdraw it, at any time. No outside identity-verification provider receives them.</li>\n<li><b>Messages, ratings and reports:</b> order-scoped chat messages, the ratings and review tags you give and receive, reports you file, and your block list.</li>\n<li><b>Emergency contacts:</b> if you add one, their name and phone number. We text them a verification code immediately, use the number only for your safety alerts, and never for marketing. If someone added you and you want the number removed, email <b>privacy@swiftgy.com</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Support and consent records:</b> your Help &amp; Support tickets, and the consent ledger — a tamper-evident record of each policy version you agreed to, when, and on what surface. At consent we may also store a keyed cryptographic digest of your network address as evidence of the event; the address itself cannot be read back from it.</li>\n<li><b>Device and diagnostics:</b> a push-notification token, app version, and crash and error reports from the app and from Swift's servers (section 5 says what they contain).</li>\n<li><b>Partner records (partners only):</b> subscription and payment-confirmation history for the weekly fee, earnings summaries, and operational quality metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2. What we do NOT collect</h2>\n<p>Swift never holds your order money, so we do not collect card numbers or mobile-money credentials. If you pay a business through MMG, that payment happens in MMG's own flow under MMG's terms — we only record that the business confirmed receiving it. Our websites and app carry no third-party advertising or cross-site tracking pixels.</p>\n<h2>3. Why we use it — and the legal bases</h2>\n<p>Under the Data Protection Act 2023, each use of your data rests on a legal basis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>To perform our contract with you:</b> running your account; transmitting orders; matching deliveries and rides; live tracking; receipts; order-scoped chat; Partner subscription administration; checking partners' identity and licence documents, and the ID a customer adds (asked for only after repeated failed deliveries or payments, or for a high-value promotion), for safety and fraud prevention.</li>\n<li><b>To meet legal obligations:</b> keeping tax and accounting records, and responding to lawful demands from authorities.</li>\n<li><b>For legitimate interests, balanced against your rights:</b> preventing fraud and abuse (strikes, collusion checks, GPS-plausibility and account-security signals), keeping the platform safe and available, and producing aggregated, de-identified statistics (e.g. orders per day) that carry no personal data. You may object — see section 9.</li>\n<li><b>With your consent, separately asked and freely refusable:</b> marketing messages; background location for movers; the optional email on your profile. Consent can be withdrawn at any time without affecting the service.</li>\n<li><b>With your explicit consent:</b> police clearance certificates (optional for taxi drivers; required for service providers who visit homes).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Automated systems and human review</h2>\n<p>Swift uses automated systems to match orders to movers, estimate times, and flag possible fraud or account-takeover risk. Flags lead to review, not to automatic punishment: no decision that produces a legal or similarly significant effect on you — losing your account, a penalty, a withheld payment confirmation — is taken by a machine alone; a human reviews the record first, and security holds (such as a staged change to where a Partner is paid) are announced to the account owner with a way to cancel them. You can contest any such decision through Help &amp; Support.</p>\n<h2>5. Sharing</h2>\n<p>A business handling your order sees the items, your first name and delivery address, and your phone number, only so it can reach you about that order. A mover handling your order sees the pickup and drop-off details, your first name and your phone number, only so they can reach you about that order; a taxi driver also sees the passenger's rating. A mover or business is never shown your last name or a photo of you. Customers see the name, photo, rating and phone number of the mover handling their order, and for a taxi the car details. When Swift's call service is switched on, the current Swift app and website stop showing these phone numbers between the people on an order, and you call each other through Swift instead: Swift rings your phone from Swift's own number and you press 1, then Swift rings the person you are calling, who must also press 1 to be connected, and neither of you sees the other's number. Older versions of the app may keep showing the number until they are retired. Calls through Swift are never recorded. For each call Swift keeps a record with no phone number in it: who asked for the call (their account, and their role on the order, such as customer, rider or store), the role they called, when, and whether it connected and for how long. If a business takes MMG, the MMG payment details it gives Swift (its MMG number or pay link, and the name on that MMG account) are shown to you on your order, so you can pay the business directly. Your verified emergency contacts receive your live location if you raise an SOS. We share data with authorities only where the law requires it, and we document every such demand.</p>\n<p><b>Technical service providers (processors):</b> to run Swift we use a small number of providers, each bound to process only what its function needs, on our instructions: DigitalOcean (hosts Swift's servers and databases, and the encrypted storage for verification documents and images, in the United States), Cloudflare (runs the domain-name service (DNS) that points Swift's web addresses to its servers), Twilio (delivers your SMS verification codes — your phone number — and, when Swift's call service is switched on, connects calls between the people on an order: both phone numbers and the call's time and length; Twilio keeps its own records of these calls, and the calls themselves are never recorded), Expo (delivers push notifications through Apple's and Google's push services — a device token), Resend (delivers our emails — your email address, if you gave one, and the email itself; it sends them from Brazil and keeps its records in the United States), GoDaddy (hosts Swift's own mailbox, which receives the emails you send us), MMG — Mobile Money Guyana — (processes a Partner's weekly-fee payment — the paying phone number and the amount), WhatsApp, run by Meta (carries the support chats you start with us on WhatsApp — your phone number and what you write), Google Places (address search, from our servers: the text you type, an approximate area to rank results, and a map pin when you look up an address; Google does not receive your network address from these searches), and the map built into your phone — Google Maps on Android, Apple Maps on iPhone — which receives the map area you are viewing in order to draw it. Routes and travel estimates run on Swift's own servers using OpenStreetMap data. On Swift's website, maps are drawn with map images from MapTiler, a map provider that uses OpenStreetMap data: to show a map, the viewer's browser requests those images from MapTiler, which receives the viewer's network (IP) address, the address of Swift's website (never the address of the page itself, so never a tracking link's code) and which map squares are shown — on a shared trip or parcel-tracking page the location is rounded before the map is drawn, and when you place a pin (a delivery address, a parcel's pickup or drop-off, or a store entrance) it is the streets around that pin. Swift sends MapTiler no name, phone number, account or order details. MapTiler's own servers may set cookies; Swift does not. Crash and error reports go to an error-tracking service (GlitchTip) that Swift runs on its own servers: for our servers' reports we attach route templates rather than full URLs, drop request query strings, headers, cookies and bodies, and run an automated filter that removes tokens and signed links from the report — a report is diagnostic context, not your account records. If the Swift phone app crashes or hits an error, it sends a crash report to the same error-tracking service (GlitchTip) on Swift's own servers: the phone model and operating system, the app version, the error and where in the app it happened, the phone's language and time zone, and a random identifier for that app install that is not linked to your account or phone number. A crash report never includes your name, phone number, messages, location or a screenshot. No outside company receives crash or error reports. Our servers are with DigitalOcean in Atlanta, United States, and several providers process data in the United States. We use each provider's written data-protection terms and send only what each needs.</p>\n<p><b>AI processing:</b> Swift sends nothing you write to an artificial-intelligence service. Search terms, store menus and your messages are handled by our own software and by people; no model reads them, and none is used to decide anything about you. Identity verification is done by our verification team: Swift uses no automated document-reading or face-matching service, and no identity-verification provider receives your documents or selfie. When you upload a document, automated security and file-format checks run on it first; they can accept the file, or reject it if it is unsafe or not a supported format. A person on our verification team then reviews each partner document and decides whether to accept it. A customer's ID goes on file at once unless a simple automatic check holds it for a person — for example, the same picture is already on another account, or the picture is too small to read; these checks never read the ID or compare faces. If a document is rejected you are told and can submit it again, or raise it through Help &amp; Support. Swift sends no other document, message or account record to any automated-analysis service.</p>\n<p><b>Business changes:</b> if Swift's business is transferred to a successor, your data moves with it under this policy's protections, and you are told before any materially different use begins.</p>\n<h2>6. Retention and deletion</h2>\n<p>We keep personal data only as long as its purpose or a legal duty requires. Verification documents are purged on a schedule after they stop being needed, with the purge itself logged; purged documents are irrecoverable. A police clearance is kept only as whether it was clear and the date it was issued: its image is deleted soon after our check (the app shows how many days before you upload it), and you can withdraw it at any time on its card in the app (Withdraw my police clearance); Swift then deletes it and keeps only a note that it was withdrawn, unless the law requires us to keep more. A customer's own ID is not on that schedule: it is kept while the account is open and deleted when the account is deleted. Order, ride and settlement history is retained for the period required for disputes, guarantees, tax and legal obligations, and order-scoped chat is retained with its order's record. The record of a call made through Swift (no phone number, never what was said) is kept with its order's record, and the record of a call you asked for is deleted when your account is deleted. If a Business records that a customer did not come to an appointment, Swift keeps that record (which booking it was, and when the Business recorded it) with its order's record, for as long as that order's record is kept, so that a person at Swift can review repeated missed appointments; it carries no fee or strike. The consent ledger is retained as legal evidence of what was agreed — it is append-only by design and is kept even after account deletion, holding only what it must. Crash and error reports are kept for 90 days, then deleted. You can delete your account from inside the app — <b>Profile → Personal data → Delete my account</b> — which closes your account, revokes signed-in access, and deletes or de-identifies personal data not subject to a legal retention duty. Customers, riders and drivers can start deletion from their profile in the app. Active jobs and unsettled cash must be resolved first; historical earnings records do not prevent deletion. If a rider still holds a customer's cash from an order, the rider's account deletion waits until our operations team records it resolved, or for at most 90 days; the cash record is kept as part of the order record. A customer's own deletion is never blocked by this. If we cannot act on a deletion request straight away, we tell you why and when, in writing, within one month. Documents under a legal or safety hold remain protected until the hold ends, and incomplete erasure is retried automatically. Business and advertiser profiles offer an in-app account closure request, tracked in Help &amp; Support, so outstanding listings, campaigns and confirmation records are handled correctly first.</p>\n<h2>7. Security</h2>\n<p>Data in transit is encrypted (the app pins Swift's certificates), access to production data is restricted and audit-logged, verification documents are encrypted at rest and readable only through short-lived signed links, security-sensitive account changes require step-up confirmation, and administrative actions on your account leave a permanent trail. If a breach is likely to put you at risk, we will tell you without undue delay and, once the Data Protection Act is in force, notify the Data Protection Commissioner within 72 hours.</p>\n<h2>8. Children</h2>\n<p>Swift is for adults: you must be 18 to hold an account, and we do not knowingly process children's data. Our published Child Safety Standards state our zero-tolerance rules and the dedicated reporting channel; anything reported there is prioritised, removed, and reported to the authorities as the law requires.</p>\n<h2>9. Your rights under, and before the start of, the Data Protection Act 2023</h2>\n<p>You have the right to: <b>access</b> the personal data we hold about you; have it <b>corrected</b>; request its <b>deletion</b>; <b>restrict</b> processing; <b>object</b> to processing based on legitimate interests and to any direct marketing; <b>withdraw a consent</b> at any time; receive a copy of your data in a <b>portable</b> form (the app's \"Download my data\" does this instantly); and <b>not be subject to a solely automated decision</b> with legal or similarly significant effect — section 4 describes how we already work that way. Exercise any of these by email to <b><a href=\"mailto:privacy@swiftgy.com\">privacy@swiftgy.com</a></b> or through Help &amp; Support; we respond within one month (up to two more months for complex requests, and we will tell you why), and identity is verified before data is released. Each right is subject to the legal retention duties described above, and exercising them never costs you the service. If you believe we have mishandled your data, you have the right to complain to the Data Protection Commissioner of Guyana.</p>\n<h2>10. Changes to this policy</h2>\n<p>Material changes are announced in the app before they take effect, and where the law requires fresh consent we ask for it. Every version of this policy is recorded with its date and an integrity hash, so the exact words that applied to you at any time are provable.</p>";

